--TEST--
Core\Db::connect names a configured block rather than a host and a password, and the second call in a request is the same connection the first opened unless the caller asks for a dedicated one — ADR 0067 §§ 2, 3, 13
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
ADR 0067 §§ 2 and 3: `connect` takes the *name* of a root-owned config block and hands back a memoized object, so `mysqli_connect`/`pg_connect` — which take the credentials as arguments and open a connection per call — have no behaviour here to be compared against.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The spec names `mysqli_connect` and `pg_connect` as what this member replaces,
// and both differ from it in the two ways that matter rather than in spelling.
// The credentials are the operator's (§ 3: an address an operator wrote into
// root-owned configuration is not attacker-influenced, which is why a named
// endpoint is pre-approved), and the object is memoized for the request (§ 13:
// nothing in the surface says whether a handshake happened).
//
// A `:memory:` database is per *connection*, so it is the sharpest instrument
// there is for asking which of the two a call answered with: a table created on
// one connection is visible on a second only if there is no second connection.
use Core\Db;

var $first = Db::connect("main");
$first->execute("create table t (id integer primary key, name text not null)", []);
$first->execute("insert into t (name) values (?)", ["ada"]);

// Same name, same request: the same connection, so the table is there.
var $again = Db::connect("main");
echo "shared sees ", $again->query("select count(*) as n from t", [])->first()?->int("n") ?? -1, "\n";

// § 2's `{shared: false}`, the other side of that bound: a dedicated connection
// for this request, which on this backend is a different `:memory:` database
// and therefore a different empty one. It is still drawn from and returned to
// § 13's pool — what it bypasses is the memoization, not the pooling.
var $dedicated = Db::connect("main", {shared: false});
try {
    var $rows = $dedicated->query("select count(*) as n from t", []);
    echo "dedicated sees ", $rows->first()?->int("n") ?? -1, "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $missing) {
    echo "dedicated sees nothing\n";
}

// And the memoized one is unaffected by the dedicated one's existence.
$first->execute("insert into t (name) values (?)", ["grace"]);
echo "shared sees ", $again->query("select count(*) as n from t", [])->first()?->int("n") ?? -1, "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# § 3: a SQLite connection's file path comes from its config block, so
# `db.connect` covers it — and this file is the whole of what the program knows
# about where the database is.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["main"]

[db.main]
driver = "sqlite"
path = ":memory:"
--EXPECT--
shared sees 1
dedicated sees nothing
shared sees 2
